7. Anger and resolution order
Ordinary arrivals start at 0 Shore Anger. Boarding starts their separate Ship Anger at 0, regardless of prior Shore Anger. Souls cannot be unloaded back onto the shore after departure.
Resolve each outward round in this order:
Optionally play one memory.
If the boat is empty, return immediately.
Advance the global round counter and add any scheduled PoLong at Ship Anger 0.
Choose a destination and calculate fog, including current PoLong penalties.
Pay Light. If it reaches 0, lose immediately.
Optionally deliver any matching passengers and receive their rewards.
Every passenger still aboard gains 1 Ship Anger, including a PoLong that arrived this round.
Each passenger reaching 4 Ship Anger becomes a wraith: lose 1 Light, then remove it.
Enforce the memory hand limit. If the boat is empty, return.
On return, each soul already waiting gains 1 Shore Anger. At 2, it becomes a wraith: lose 1 Light, then remove it. Resolve this before adding new arrivals.
Wraiths leave permanently and have no continuing fog effect.
8. Mother and Child quest
Deliver Child to Haven before delivering Mother to Tartarus. The deliveries may happen in different cycles.
When Mother is delivered after Child, receive both:
Mother’s normal Restore Light memory.
One special Passage memory.
Passage counts toward the hand limit and the one-memory use limit. It instantly delivers one soul currently aboard, including PoLong, to its preferred destination.
That delivery gives its normal reward and counts toward the winning target if it is an ordinary soul. It costs no fog and advances no anger or round counter.
If Passage empties the boat, return immediately. Passage has already used the memory allowance for that return.
Delivering Mother first, or losing either family member to a wraith before their required delivery, makes the quest unavailable. The quest awards Passage only once.
9. Light, Haven and returning
Starting Light: 6.
Maximum Light: 6.
Return travel costs 0 Light and is not an outward round.
Resolve waiting souls’ Shore Anger and wraith losses first.
If still alive, recover 2 Light, capped at 6.
Record the completed cycle and check whether the game ends.
If another cycle remains, refill the shore and generate its map.
Ordinary deliveries do not automatically restore Light. Mother’s memory provides recovery when played.
Haven has 0 base fog, but does not heal. PoLong can still add fog there. Replace “free rest point” with “zero-base-fog destination.”
Wording and example
Use “round” consistently for outward travel and “cycle” for a complete trip. Replace “one soul of each memory type” with “one of each listed ordinary soul.” State that Child and PoLong give no memory.
Example: Before global round 6, Light is 4, Keeper and Soldier each have Ship Anger 3, and the player holds a Fog Shield. Play it, then add the scheduled PoLong at anger 0. Choose Asphodel: base fog 2, minus 1 protection, leaves Light 3. Deliver Keeper and receive its memory. Soldier reaches anger 4, becomes a wraith, and reduces Light to 2. PoLong reaches anger 1 and remains aboard, so the cycle continues.

Game Guide Demo
This is a Paper Demo Guideline. 
Premise
You play Charon's apprentice. Your job is to ferry souls across the river and drop each one off where it wants to go.
Key terms
Round: one stop on the river. The river splits into 2 destinations, and you travel to one of them.
Cycle: one full trip. It starts when you pick up souls at the shore and ends when your boat is empty. Then you return to the shore for new arrivals.
Light: your health. If light reaches 0, you lose. Start at 6.
Fog: each destination has a fog value. When you arrive there, you lose that much Light.
Seats: your boat has 4 seats. Most souls take 1 seat, and some take 2.
Anger: how long a soul has been kept waiting. Too much Anger turns a soul into a wraith (see Anger below).
Memory: a card you earn by delivering a soul to its preferred destination. Each memory has a special effect.
How a cycle plays
New map. A river map with a set path is randomly generated for this cycle. 
Choose passengers. At the shore, fill up to 4 seats with souls. Each soul has a preferred destination, and you need to deliver all of them before they turn into wraiths. Souls you don't pick stay on the shore.
Pick a destination. At the start of each round, choose 1 of 2 destinations. When you arrive, you lose Light equal to that destination's fog.
Drop off souls (optional). A soul can only be dropped off at its preferred destination. When you drop one off, you get its memory. You can also keep a soul on board and drop it off later, for example to complete a quest in the right order.
End of round. Every soul still on your boat gains +1 Ship Anger.
Repeat steps 3–5 until your boat is empty.
Return to the shore. Every soul still waiting on the shore gains +1 Shore Anger. New souls arrive, and the next cycle starts from step 1.






Anger
Souls that wait too long turn into wraiths. When a soul becomes a wraith, you lose 1 Light and the soul leaves the game.


